<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lenca people</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lenca are an indigenous people of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a> and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-salvador">El Salvador</a>, living mainly in the highland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> of Intibucá, Lempira and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-paz">La Paz</a> in Honduras. Their language died out in the twentieth century, but they keep distinctive traditions of pottery and ritual. The sixteenth-century leader Lempira, who resisted the Spanish conquest, became a Honduran national hero.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lenca-people.jpg" alt="Lenca people" width="320" height="175" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: COAmaker17 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lenca_Flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Lenca have lived in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-salvador">El Salvador</a> since before the arrival of Europeans. The site of Quelepa in eastern El Salvador, inhabited from the preclassic period to the early postclassic, is thought to have been a Lenca settlement, and archaeologists speculate that it was settled by Lenca speakers from Honduras. Yarumela in the Comayagua Valley was a large centre in the late preclassic and early classic periods and probably an important place of trade. The name Lenca is of unknown origin; Efraim Squier used it in 1859 as a term for all the subgroups.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Lenca were divided into subgroups with their own dialects, among them the Care, Cerquín, Potón, Taulepa, Colo, Ulua and Xucap. At the start of the 16th century each subgroup formed a confederation ruled by a supreme lord and divided into manors and towns. A town's head man was assisted by four lieutenants and was succeeded by his first-born or chosen by the clan mothers. Towns stood in valleys or on fortified crags and were built mostly of adobe.</p><p>Society was divided into nobility, including priests and justices, and commoners, who paid tribute in honey and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> cloaks. Commoners grew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassava">cassava</a>, sweet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potato</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacao">cacao</a>, beans, squash and cotton, and hunted deer, armadillo and wild turkey. The Lenca used a version of the 365-day Mesoamerican calendar of 18 months of twenty days. At certain times of year the lordships agreed truces, remembered in the guancasco, an annual ceremony in which neighbouring communities confirm peace and friendship before the statue of a town's patron saint.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The population in the early 16th century may have exceeded 500,000. Lempira's war of resistance against the Spanish lasted about twelve years and ended with his death in 1537. Disease brought from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> cut numbers sharply: one estimate gives 300,000 Lenca in the 1520s and about 25,000 in 1550. The Lenca were converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>, often by force, and came to speak Spanish.</p><p>In 1993 the Lenca activist Berta Cáceres co-founded the Civic Council of Popular and Indigenous Organizations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a> (COPINH). She opposed the Agua Zarca dam on the Gualcarque River, won the Goldman Environmental Prize in 2015 and was murdered at her home on 3 March 2016. Lenca women today make distinctive pottery, notably in La Campa near Gracias.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lenca” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the nineteenth century most people were mestizo smallholders and cattle ranchers in the interior, with <b>Lenca</b> and other indigenous communities in the west, Garífuna and English-speaking Bay Islanders on the Caribbean coast, and Miskitu in the eastern Mosquitia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-honduras">Republic of Honduras · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
